<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>2.2.3: Graphs of Systems of Equations</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00391</md:content-id>
    <md:title>2.2.3: Graphs of Systems of Equations</md:title>
    <md:uuid>0dc8b014-89a5-40be-967b-d6ebe00c396f</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <para>Now that you have written equations from tables, it is time to write systems of equations from graphs.</para>
      <para>
        <emphasis>Part 1: Writing a System of Equations from a Given Graph
  </emphasis>
     </para>
      <para>For questions 1 – 8, use the given graph:</para>
      <figure>
        <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_04_06_01.jpg"/>
        </media>
      </figure>
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <title>Student Activity</title>
        <exercise id="_0a49b738-8436-497d-8638-18fa613b6ced" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para> For the green dashed line what is the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! 9</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 9.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="ec23dfc4-4e5e-4dfc-b5a8-854b9fbd123c" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para> For the green dashed line what is the slope?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! -1</para>
            <para>The correct answer is -1.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Q#3-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para> What is the equation of the green dashed line in slope-intercept form?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(y= -x+9\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="adafd334-fdb7-43a1-aa7d-69c9835cde5a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para> For the orange solid line what is the \(y\)-value of the \(y\)-intercept?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>Nice try! The correct answer is 5.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="e5b6a568-4cef-49f8-a35b-df8ac3233ff4" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            
            <para> For the orange solid line what is the slope?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct!</para>
            <para>The correct answer is 3.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
      <!--Q#6-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para> What is the equation of the orange solid line in slope-intercept form?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(y = 3x +5\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#7-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para> Write the two equations as a system of linear equations.</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(y= -x+9\)</para>
          <para>\(y = 3x +5\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#8-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para> What is the solution of the system of equations?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\((1, 8)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para>
        <emphasis>Part 2: Use a Graph to Solve a Given System</emphasis>
     </para>
      <para>Now that you wrote a system of equations from a given graph, how can you work backward and graph a given system of equations by hand? Discuss this with a partner.</para>
      <para>For questions 1 - 2, use this system of equations:</para>
      <para>\(y=4x-4\)</para>
      <para>\(y= -2x+8\)</para>
      <!--Q#1-->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
        <item> On the same coordinate plane, graph \(y = 4x -4\) and \(y = -2x +8\) by hand.
 </item>
      </list>
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <!--QUESTION IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <figure>
              <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_04_06_02.jpg"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#2-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para> What is the solution to the system of equations?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\((2, 4)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para>For numbers 3 and 4, consider the system of equations:</para>
      <para>\(y = - \frac{3}{4}x+5\)</para>
      <para>\(y=\frac{1}{2}x-5\)</para>
      <!--Q#3-->
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
        <item> On the same coordinate plane, graph the system of equations by hand.</item>
      </list>
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <!--QUESTION IS OUTSIDE OF INTERACTION DUE TO DESMOS-->
            <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Compare your answer:</para>
            <figure>
              <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_02_04_06_03.jpg"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Q#3-->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para> What is the solution to the system of equations?</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\((8, -1)\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <title>Video: Graphing a System of Equations</title>
      <para>Watch the following video to learn more about writing systems of equations from graphs.</para>
      <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
        <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/RQHhdqkJEo0?si=lGR1PpEOg-oREXv2;&amp;rel=0"/>
      </media>
      <!-- "Start "Are you Ready for More? click to reveal.  If you have more than one on a page, you will need to change the Data-fire/data-wait-for-events for each set.-->
      <example>
        <exercise>
          <problem>
            <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
            <!-- INSERT ANY VALID HTML HERE -->
          </problem>
        </exercise>
      </example>
      <!--Start interaction.  If multiple interactions appear under "are you ready for more?, they should all have matching "data-wait-for-event", which should also match the "data-fire-event" for the button. Note in this sample, they are all RFM1-->
      <exercise class="RFM1">
        <problem>
          <title>Extending Your Thinking</title>
          <para>Graph the following system of equations on a coordinate plane:</para>
          <para>\(y=2x +5\)</para>
          <para>\(4x-2y =10\)</para>
          <para>Find the solution.</para>
          <para>(Hint: For the second equation, solve for \(y\) first.)</para>
          
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>Infinitely many solutions. When the second equation is solved for \(y\), it becomes \(-2y =-4x=10\) then \(y = 2x +5\). Both equations are the same. So, on the graph, one line is on top of the other and all solutions are possible.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--End interaction-->
    </section>
  </content>
